---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Architecture" description="amber architecture, C++17 bare-metal agent harness">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Architecture</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        amber is a C++17 agent harness, not a Python wrapper, not a Node.js script, not a React
        frontend with a Flask backend. Compiled C++ with libcurl, ncurses, and zero Python dependency.
      </p>
      <p>
        The engine is a portable library, <code>libagent_core.a</code>, with no UI and no
        main(). The three binaries are thin shells over it: <code>amber</code> (interactive
        TUI), <code>amber-cli</code> (headless, for workflows and scheduling), and
        <code>amber-bench</code> (benchmark and KPI harness). Embed the library in your own
        C++ project and you get the agent loop, tool registry, compression pipeline, and MCP
        transport without a terminal in sight.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>┌─ libagent_core.a ──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Agent loop · LLM client · Tool registry · Compression    │
│  Context integrity (FNV-1a hash chain) · MCP transport     │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         │              │              │
    ┌────▼─────┐  ┌────▼─────┐  ┌────▼─────┐
    │ amber    │  │amber-cli │  │amber-bench│
    │ (TUI)    │  │(headless)│  │(KPI harness)│
    └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Context Integrity</h2>
      <p>
        The Context class uses an FNV-1a hash chain to seal every message. Once on the stack,
        messages can never be modified in-place. The only operations are push (append), pop
        (remove top, LIFO), clear, and get_all (read-only). get_all() recomputes the entire
        chain and asserts the final hash matches chain_hash_. Any in-place modification of a
        sealed message breaks every subsequent link and triggers an assertion.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>h_0 = FNV(0              || msg_0)
h_1 = FNV(h_0            || msg_1)
h_2 = FNV(h_1            || msg_2)
...
chain_hash_ = h_n-1</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Compression Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        Two-phase pipeline: classify-then-extract. Phase 1 classifies the conversation into
        core/context/prune segments. Phase 2 extracts memory and skill operations. The pipeline
        is pure, the context is only READ. Classify/extract requests are assembled from a
        working copy of the snapshot, so a failed pipeline leaves the context untouched by
        construction.
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Classify: segments the conversation by importance</li>
        <li>Extract: pulls memory/skill ops from the LLM</li>
        <li>Prune: replaces bulky tool outputs with placeholders</li>
        <li>Headroom: enforces 25% headroom after compression</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Tool Registry</h2>
      <p>
        Tools are registered by the host (library or UI) and looked up by name when the model
        requests an invocation. Each tool has a name, description, parameter schema, and an
        execute method. The bash tool is approval-gated, each call must be approved by the
        host before it runs. If no approval handler is installed the call is denied (fail-safe).
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>Tool interface:
  name() → string
  description() → string
  parameters_schema() → json
  requires_approval(args) → bool
  is_read_only() → bool
  execute(arguments) → ToolResult</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Security Model</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Workspace confinement: read/write confine paths to the workspace root</li>
        <li>Approval gating: bash tool requires explicit user approval</li>
        <li>Process isolation: commands run in their own process group</li>
        <li>Output capping: command output capped at 64 KiB</li>
        <li>Timeout: default 60s timeout on shell commands</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Compression Observer</h2>
      <p>
        CompressionObserver is notified of compression pipeline progress. All methods have
        default no-op implementations so consumers only override what they need. The UI can
        display live progress during compression.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>CompressionObserver interface:
  on_compress_start(msgs_before, tokens_before)
  on_loop_collapse(removed)
  on_llm_request_sent()
  on_llm_reply_received(elapsed_ms)
  on_parse_result(compression_response)
  on_apply_result(compression_result)
  on_error(msg)
  on_compress_done(compression_result)</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Sub-Agent Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        SubAgentExecutor runs focused sub-agents with their own context and a custom system
        prompt. Execution mode is runtime-configurable:
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Serial: sub-agents run one at a time, sequential requests share the system-prompt prefix, keeping provider prompt caches warm</li>
        <li>Parallel: sub-agents run concurrently, capped by max()</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build System</h2>
      <p>Standard configure + make. The GNUmakefile auto-runs ./configure to generate Makefile from Makefile.in:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>make          # build everything (lib + cli + tui)
make test     # run unit tests
make lint     # run clang-tidy
make analyze  # run cppcheck
make clean    # remove .o / .d / binaries
make distclean # also removes the generated Makefile</pre>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
